> Nifty little challenge.

I would say only exclude the items definately considered animal 
products. If you don't know for sure, allow it. The only gloves 
definately animal are the leather ones. Nonmetallic doesn't mean 
leather. For boots, exclude low and high. There are possibly some 
descriptions to exclude as well... riding? Because any description could 
be 'kicking', which is certainly metallic, it implies the descriptions 
alone don't necessitate leather.

Also, would vegan lifestyle include vegan diet? Kind of strange to not 
use animal products, except to devour their flesh.

Then again, kind of strange for a vegan to slaughter hundreds of animals 
and then just leave the corpses there to rot.

> 
> Ivory rings could not be worn, nor could candles be used.  Leather drums
> and drums of earthquakes are out of the question, so are oilskin
> sacks[2].  Oil lamps could not be used, neither could magic lamps or,
> probably, brass lanterns (its going to be a dark trip).  Potions of oil
> are excluded, and I would exclude "milky" potions as well.  Hope that
> leather spellbook wasn't a spell you needed.  Saddles and leashes are
> gone as well.  

Other tools would be horns, can of grease, and debatably a figurine.

Lamps and lanterns should be fine, as someone else pointed out.

Leashes are not necessarily leather, so should be allowed. It may be an 
anachronism to consider cloth leashes (hemp is reasonable though), but 
Nethack doesn't exactly worry about anachronisms.

> 
> Whips and slingshots are out of the question, but that shouldn't cause
> too much sufferings.  Worm teeth and crysknives should also be excluded, 
> IMNSHO.

Many or most weapons probably have a leather grip, btw. I agree that 
just the ones you mention should be off limits though (since we don't 
/know/).
> 
> Unihorns, being bone, is definitely out of the question, and that should
> really, really hurt.

This brings up a question I've had for a while. What exactly is 
"applying" a unihorn? If it's rubbing it, or drinking from it, then yes, 
it should be off limits. But I always thought of it as a sort of prayer, 
focused on the purity of a unicorn, to purify yourself. If that's the 
case, it seems like you'd be honoring the memory of the creature, and so 
it should be allowed for vegans.

One last item you left out: you shouldn't be able to feed pets 
meat/animal products either, since that's using it. So no training 
carnivores to steal.

To make true vegan, it'd have to encompass the lifestyle, the diet, and 
maybe pacifism? Or it could be a reverse vegan diet, where you have to 
eat any animal you kill (so the animal's life wasn't wasted). This 
wouldn't be an issue for pacifists, but it would be an interesting 
challenge.

